4434	MUCH safer and easier on the body and mind than crutches.  More convenient, too, you can carry something WHILE moving around!  And unlike crutches, it sits there, ready and able when you get-up from sitting and need it.<br /><br />DUAL HAND BRAKES are a major bonus for user safety.  And on my hardwood and solid flooring, I can attest to the importance of having right and left rear wheel braking!<br /><br />I chose this model for its nice size, stability, portability and dual hand/safety brakes. Wondered about my selection when a piece from the steering column broke off while doing the very minimal assembly immediately out of the box.  But still working great after using for three months.<br /><br />Had SEVERE discomfort due to the poorly designed and engineered knee pad (as in nearly crippling pain after just tinkering around for about 5 minutes one day, ahead of scheduled surgery). The pad is quite unforgiving (hard) and shaped so that no readily available gel pad on the market can be employed to resolve the discomfort issues.  Bought a sheepskin pad (suggested with this item), but returned it without ever opening.  NO such thing will help make that insufferable knee pad any more bearable.  I layered some old mattress-topper foam, centered it widthwise inside a pillow case, then safety-pinned the ends of the pillowcase together under the knee pad frame at front & back.  A definite improvement.  Ahhh, no further knee pain -- and no hint of the skin \\\\"leathering\\\\" problems some others have mentioned.<br /><br />I've seen a Roscoe Medical Knee Walkers in use by folks wearing a cast on the lower leg (it comes with a well-positioned basket), and boy does that thing have NICE cushions!!  One major drawback of the Roscoe unit: it has bolts protruding from the wheel axles that users have sliced the ankle of their good leg on while in use.  With my primary concern being safety, I selected the Drive Medical unit over the Roscoe for its dual hand brakes.<br /><br />At 6' tall I'm using the next to lowest setting. People under 5'8\\\\" may have a tough time getting the proper height on that knee pad. I've seen a number of reviews commenting on not being able to adjust it low enough for users within the advertised height range.<br />I'm 160 lbs, have been doing core-strengthening as part of physical therapy for a few months, and feel perfectly stable on the knee walker in either direction: forward or backward (which is good, as it takes ROOM to turn the unit around).<br /><br />Bought the Sunlight mesh-bottom basket (recommended as an add-on by Amazon), thinking it would make it easier to carry a book and water from here to there, but it protrudes well beyond the handlebar span and steering column, further reducing clearance for turning and might bang into walls and appliances. I'm not going to use the basket on the walker, but will keep to use on a bicycle. The rack for that basket, however, affixed to the steering bar and column, is useful to safely and quickly hang a tote bag (for phones/Kindle/iPad/etc.). That's a help.	\N	1	POSITIVE	4	1	\N	4434	132	\N	\N
