7324	The unit is good, does not cost too much (both up front and to operate (but that's still under debate)), requires daily checking/manitenance (as do all tubs), fits even big people (I'm 6'4\\\\"), fits compact corner areas, small enough to lessen cleaning time and refill water quantity. The unit is Ozone ready, which only means that it has a molex plug for power activated during the filtration cycle and a hose/jet to allow ozone to flow into the tub. The unit is solidly built but not too heavy; once delivered, I moved the unit in place by myself with only the help of one four wheel furniture dolly. Requires a 220V hook up, which is the biggest pain since copper is so expensive. The wiring alone cost $150; and mine was a short < 50' run. Add to the that a local cutoff switch ($15), legal, and GFCI breaker ($80) and you've got $250 without labor; which I provided. Oh yeah, the 6 guage wire required me to purchase heavy duty cable cutters ($25) just to cut the wire; I did not have or know what else to use. Then comes the chemicals. This is definitely not a one time cost. If you have many friends, this will quickly become too small a tub.     The bad:    A bit noisy when operating, but negligable while in the tub. The problem comes when it's in circulation mode only, and you're not in the tub, the pump still runs at full speed; does not use a two stage pump. This makes the standard filtering cycle bothersome if you are sensitive to noisy things being noisy for no good reason. I can hear in the front of my house with all the doors and windows shut.    The filter basket and filter element constantly bob to the top of the water level when the pump is not sucking them down. This means that when the next circulation cycle starts, the filter and basket get sucked down and don't always go down straight. This meanse that they get stuck cock-eyed and put stress on themselves and the housing as well as let some stuff possibly get through to the motor. The suction is fairly strong. Nolocking ring or screw method is present to secure either piece. This is a major design flaw.     The jets are plentiful, but a little underpowered. The 4hp motor sounds good, but I think the 30 jets may need a little more umpf. Also, the jets themselves are a little cheap. If you screw them too hard (they adjust the force of each jet, which is actually nice!) or pull out too much, they can literally pop out in your hand. The one that did this just that for me poped back in and is holding, but I expect to replace one or more in the near future. This is a problem that I've read about on other Keys reviews.    The provided cover, although it doees keep stuff out, should be replaced by a hard cover. The soft cover has a tendency to drain into the tub if not lifted by two people and very carefully. This then dumps in some of the stuff that it was intended to keep out.     Keys water hot and has multiple methods for saving money or keeping temperature constant. So far, it's been a good experience and great to be in.	\N	19	POSITIVE	4	19	\N	7324	132	\N	\N
