9068	A few years ago, started having some major problems with sciatica.  I went to physical therapy and the therapist prescribed a TENS unit.  Though not a miracle cure for the sciatica, the TENS unit (Flex-It) did seem to help significantly with pain management.  When I received the prescription unit, I asked about cost to me, explaining I had researched the units and could buy one for about $100 (they are cheaper now).  The therapist checked and I was told that insurance would cover the unit.  I also started getting supplies in the mail from the company providing the TENS.  Being somewhat suspicious, I didn't use most of the supplies and put them away.  Some time later (maybe as much as a year), I received a bill for my co-pay of $300+.  The copay for the TENS unit was over $100 plus they were billing me for all the supplies.  The most outrageous cost being for a 3 ounce bottle of pretty generic after lotion.  My copay for this lotion was $3 a bottle and they billed the insurance company $11.  The lotion was something that could have been purchased in the dollar store.  No wonder our health care costs are so high!  In the end, I never paid anything for the stuff as I threatened to send it all back and notify the insurance company.  I also knew I had never signed a contract and I asked them to send me a copy of any contract I had signed agreeing to the pay for the supplies and TENS unit.  In any case, it was a huge hassle.  When my unit died, I did not want to go through insurance again and purchased this unit.<br /><br />This unit is a little less adjustable than the prescription TENS.  It has plenty of different stimulator modes but you can't adjust the modes individually. I don't miss the adjustments, I never really found the adjust-ability of the pulse lengths to be particularly useful on the Flex-It model.  The Flex-It model is a little heavier and seems to have a better build quality, but it did die after a few years.  The Top Tens model actually has a stronger electrical pulse than the prescription TENS.  I am not even close to maximum power on it.  The Top Tens unit also uses regular AAA batteries rather than proprietary batteries used in the prescription unit.  The prescription unit did recharge but you couldn't use it when it was plugged in.  So, if it died you had to wait for it to recharge before you used it.  With the Top TENS unit, I can use rechargeable AAA batteries and switch them out when they die.  The TOP TENS also runs considerably longer on a charge.  The prescription unit only lasted a couple of hours.  I can't tell you how long the TOP TENS works but I've used the unit quite a few hours without having to replace the batteries.  The TOP TENS unit does take the same electrodes as my prescription model, which is nice as I still have a bunch of them.<br /><br />These machines really do help with pain.  Recently I've been using it for shoulder and neck pain.  When researching units, I found that many do force you to use the same output strength on all electrodes.  This unit has two channels and lets you adjust output for them individually.  If you have pain on things like shoulder and neck, this is important.  My neck can't take nearly as high a setting as my lower shoulder or my back.  We will see how long this unit holds up.  It is pretty light weight and plasticy, but light weight also has it's advantages if you want to clip it to your belt and wear it while you are doing things.  It is every bit as effective as the prescription unit I had, without the insurance scams!	\N	4	POSITIVE	5	4	\N	9068	132	\N	\N
