13887	A couple weeks ago, I started having severe back pain.  In my quest to reduce the discomfort I decided it was time to explore a massage chair (and not the cheapy ones that just buzz).<br /><br />I had seen chairs at stores like Sharper Image and BrookStone in the mall, so I thought those places were a good place to begin my search...<br /><br />The first thing I noticed is to get a good quality chair, you have to spend a minimum of nearly $2000.  That number alone was eye popping, as I really thought that the minimum, was going to be my maximum.<br /><br />To that end, I went to The Sharper Image first, as they have a $1800 model called the Interactive Health \\\\"Human Touch\\\\" massage chair.  I can't tell you how much I wanted to like this one, as it was priced in my range, had decent looks and it had a few features that were not available on other chairs in the sub $2500 class like heat and an independantly controllable ottoman with leg pressure cuffs that reverses when not in use to make the chair more presentable for times when the massager is not being used...  Unfortunately the chair was a bust.  The massage was so overly agressive that it felt like someone was placing unpadded plastic rollers all down my back with no \\\\"human touch\\\\" at all.  I find the name to be somewhat of an oxymoron, as the touch was *very* mechanical, and uncomfortable.  Also, I found the remote very confusing, and adjustments for height and size were all manual and difficult to pin down.  Bottom line is it was not the right chair for me.<br /><br />Disappointed after my SI visit, I walked over to BrookStone to try a Panasonic model called \\\\"The Lifestyle\\\\" massager at $2000.  This chair looked great on the web, and Panasonic has a great reputation in the massage chair arena.  Unfortunately for me, it looks like BrookStone is phasing out the Panasonic line from their stores as they were aquired by OSIM about a year ago.  The sales rep said for the record that they are not phasing out the line due to the acquisition, but rather because they feel the OSIM brand is superior... OK, I will let you draw your own conclusion there.  Anyways the Lifestyle chair was unavailable for me to try, so I could only test the entry level OSIM model called the iMedic 380 at $2300.<br /><br />As the 380 was above my price range, I went into the test with a large bit of skepticism (thinking I would just buy the Panasonic somewhere else, or from the BrookStone web site where it can still be purchased currently).  However, since I was in the store anyway, I figured \\\\"why not?\\\\".<br /><br />Well, the quick review is I *loved* the 380.  I initially was put off by the looks of the chair (coupled with the aformentioned price) as it was an ugly light brown in faux leather that would fool no one (black is also available).  It also looked very much like a massage chair, and not like the beautiful pictures of the Panasonic I saw on the web.  My negative attitude quickly diminished when the sales rep turned the chair on and the massage began...  The first thing the chair does is go down your entire back and scans your body.  This is to check to see how tall you are as well as body width, so the chair can focus on massaging the right places (something both the Panasonic and Interactive Health both lack).  Then the massage began, and unlike the Interactive Health \\\\"Human Touch\\\\" chair that felt anything but, the OSIM felt like a real human masseuse was doing the massage.  It was much smoother and less agressive *feeling*, but much *more* effective.  Obviously that body scanning technology works!  I finished the test with the massage turned off to see if the chair felt good when used without the massage, and it absolutely does.  Needless to say I am sold, and I am going to buy one.<br /><br />The bottom line is I guess in this case, price and effectiveness seem to go hand and hand.  While the Panasonic may have also impressed me if I could have tried it, the OSIM's body scanning technology won meover and will get me to fork over the extra dough for it.  I highly recommend the iMedic 380 for fellow back pain sufferers as it was able to eliminate mine that day.  Now I just need to count down the days until it is in my home.over and will get me to fork over the extra dough for it.  I highly recommend the iMedic 380 for fellow back pain sufferers as it was able to eliminate mine that day.  Now I just need to count down the days until it is in my home.	\N	23	POSITIVE	5	24	\N	13887	132	\N	\N
