14178	Beware of this crappy product. The product has some serious engineering flaws, and the customer service to go with it is poor.<br /><br />I spent over $600 to buy this base station, an answering machine, and several expandable handsets. Problems include:<br /><br />1. BIG PROBLEM: After you set up the phone, it seems to be fine for a week or two, and then it mysteriously quits working. The symptoms are: a) The user does not get dial tone; b) If someone tries to call, the base station does not \\\\"see\\\\" the incoming call so the phone doesn't ring and the display on the handsets does not show it. If you call the customer service line, they will tell you to remove the batteries from the base station and then put them back in. The batteries are hot. According to an electronics engineer who examined the phone for me, he says the phone goes into a state in which the circuitry is not working properly, which heats up the batteries, which makes the phone quit working. He said the purpose of removing the batteries and putting them back in is to let the batteries cool down, and to let the circuitry reset itself. (My question to him had been, \\\\"Would using electrical tape to hold the batteries in position help?\\\\" and his answer was \\\\"No, because the batteries slipping out of position is not the problem.\\\\") Let me just say that asking my 89-year-old technophobe mother to weekly remove her stupid phone's batteries and then put them back in is not a feasible solution. And since I live 100 miles from her, it's not exactly feasible for me to come by and do it for her every week. Of course, customer service never admitted this circuitry problem to me - they tried to claim it was my fault for placing the batteries incorrectly into the battery compartment in the first place. So they lied to me about what the root cause of the problem was, and failed to admit it was incompetent design on their side.<br /><br />2. ANOTHER BIG PROBLEM: Customer service is as crappy as the product. They only workMonday through Friday from 8 am to 5 pm during Pacific time zone. My 89-year-old mother is in Central time zone, which means she (and her assisted living workers) must wait until after 10 am to call. Even if you call these customer disservice people after 8 am their time, they still won't necessarily answer the phone. I called at 8:15 am their time and got voice mail. I left a message with my contact info, and nobody called me back. I called again and got some guy who told me I had to remove the batteries and put them back in, which was a problem since I was 100 miles away. So I asked him to call an assisted living contact for me, someone who had agreed to go through the process with him - he promised to do that, but then he didn't call. I called a third time, and had to leave another message that never got returned. The fourth call, I finally got someone who did follow up and call the assisted living contact for me. It's ridiculous that it took 4 calls to customer disservice before I finally got someone who would call the assisted living contact and instruct her on how to troubleshoot this thing.<br /><br />3. A THIRD BIG PROBLEM. Sometimes the phone goes into a weird state in which the handset says LINE BUSY - except, the line is NOT busy. In this situation, if you press the TALK button, you hear nothing on the phone. It takes several iterations of alternating between the TALK button and the red hangup button before the LINE BUSY message clears and the phone is usable. My 89-year-old mother does NOT have the capability to deal with this crap. The stupid phone should work, it shouldn't go into these non-functional states.<br /><br />4. Each handset much be individually programmed, one at a time. Other cordless phone systems have a feature that let you program one handset, then propagate its settings to all the others, but not this one. So if you want to load the user's phone book into the handsets, that means keying in each phone number into each handset individually, one at a time. If you have 20 phone numbers to enter and 5 handsets, that means you have over 100 phone numbers and names to enter. The same is true for all the settings of handset volume, ring tones, and everything else that's programmable in the handsets. Other manufacturers give the user a choice - they let you either propagate settings from one handset to everything else, or have each handset behave uniquely. Not so with Serene - your only choice is the labor-intensive situation that I doubt any of their customers actually WANT to do.<br /><br />5. The instructions for preparing the photo buttons for the speed dial are horribly incomplete. The instructions tell you they provide a template, but this \\\\"template\\\\" is nothing more than a piece of cardboard that has squares on it the size of the push buttons. There is nothing in the documentation telling you the measurements of the push-buttons to guide you in how to size your photos, and for those of us who start with digital photos from our phone cameras or digital cameras, there is no guidance on how to resize those photos on the computer (ie, pixel dimensions) in order to make them the right size for the phone. You basically have to take their stupid printed piece of cardboard and measure it for yourself, and then figure out for yourself how to translate that to pixels for your photo editing software. The other thing the instructions don't tell you is that the keycaps for the push buttons have very large numbers on them, for the purpose of allowing those same keys to be used for dialing numeric phone numbers. The instructions don't warn you to leave \\\\"negative space\\\\" (background) on the photo to accommodate these large numbers - so you end up with a huge number displayed on top of a family member's face. The not-so-helpful cardboard template lacks this information as well.<br /><br />6. Programming this thing and its handsets is complicated. It's impossible to do it without the written instructions. I work in the high-tech industry, and I've figured out a lot of technology in my day without needing the manual, but with this crappy phone you're totally lost if you don't have the manual right in front of you to tell you what to do. There's absolutely nothing intuitive about the user interface of the setup process of pairing the handsets, programming the handsets, or programming the base station.	\N	1	NEGATIVE	1	1	\N	14178	132	\N	\N
14262	At the time I bought this item, I didn't even know I was buying a \\\\"solar charger\\\\":  I thought I was buying a SteriPEN! (see 'long story', below).      However, now that I have it, I'll say it's a decent solar charger for rechargeable lithium batteries.  I only have the one, so I can't compare it to others; but it works as well as one could expect for a solar charger of this size.  It takes about 3 days of bright sun to get a nearly full charge.  If you're not afield, you can use the supplied wall-charger, which will charge 2 Lion batteries in 6 hours or so.    One warning:  Although it seems very water-resistant, if it gets rained on, it will leak through the charger port.  So you might want to tape that up if you're using the charger outside.  In other words, tape it up!    In partial sun, I measured 14V @ 60mA into a 0.56 ohm load, or about 840 milliwatts.  The box itself is about 3.5x9x17cm, but the active solar cell area is about 14x6cm.  Since the solar constant is about 1kW/m2, that puts the efficiency at about 10%, which is about right for mono-crystalline cells.      ===============================  The \\\\"Long Story\\\\" (old)  ===============================  The below review was written several years ago, when 'ActiveForever' was still selling this solar charger, but showing a picture of a SteriPEN, instead.  So for many months, people (myself included) bought what they thought was a SteriPEN; only to find out later what they had REALLY being sold!     And though I tried for FOUR MONTHS to get Amazon.com or ActiveForever to fix the product image, the fraud continued!  I guess they didn't care, because the \\\\"error\\\\"(?) meant they sold a WHOLE BUNCH of \\\\"solar chargers\\\\" to people who thought they were buying a Steripen.      But, finally Amazon, or ActiveForever, changed the fraudulent product illustration.  Yes, ActiveForever's sales of the \\\\"solar chargers\\\\" have likely since dropped off, but they must be doing OK, since they're still  paying for an \\\\"Amazon.com sponsored link\\\\".    And since the below 'review' is no longer relevant -- now that the fraud's been ended -- I was about to delete the whole thing.     But then I decided it might be good to keep around for a while, to serve as a reminder to myself and maybe as a warning to others to be careful.       ==================================  Original \\\\"Review\\\\" (aka: warning!)  ==================================  I don't know how long this will stay posted, because Amazon.com seems to delete this review as fast as I post it.   And they delete the picture of the actual product that's for sale here, nearly as soon as I've uploaded it.  This is my second try to warn people.     So if you can read this, you probably just saved yourself $59.  But you'd better read fast!     In 25 words or less, the SteriPEN Adventurer is a very good product.  But the item for sale here has nothing to do with a SteriPEN.  This is actually a listing for a Solar Battery Charger for the SteriPEN!     So, read the full product title -- all the way to the end.  Pay particular notice of the last two words -- \\\\"Solar Charger\\\\" -- because that's all your going to hear about a \\\\"solar charger\\\\", until you open your order from Active Forever, and see what your $59 actually bought you.     And if you end up, as I did, as a proud owner of a solar charger for the SteriPEN, rather than the owner of an actual SteriPEN,  take comfort in knowing you aren't the only one, and now have a really nice solar battery charger.        However, I'm fairly certain this charger won't keep you safe from Giardia, or any other water-borne parasite, unless you are really agile and can manage to squish the little buggers with it.   So practice your backhand!	\N	2	NEGATIVE	1	7	\N	14262	132	\N	\N
