755	I read the other reviews before writing this & I can understand perfectly why the other reviewers made the criticisms they did & why they had the problems they described. This wax IS a little tricky to use at first, but once you have mastered it, it works b-e-a-u-t-i-f-u-l-l-y! My 11-year old daughter has developed hairy lower legs in the last year & has been begging to have them waxed, as I used to do to my own before the \\\\"miracle of electrolysis\\\\"! When I remembered how frequently I managed to overheat the conventional wax for myself using the conventional method & how difficult it was to remove the little bits of wax that are usually left clinging to the skin, I decided to try the Nad's, because (a) it is used at room temperature & (b) all residue dissolves immediately & competely in water. My daughter & I spent an hour \\\\"perfecting\\\\" our technique & the learning curve was enormous, but by the end, we were *really* ripping! The next time I did her legs we managed to do them *both* in about 20 minutes. Here are my tips to use Nads with terrific results: (1) Spread the wax on in a Very, Very Thin Layer. Repeat: Very, Very THIN!!! When first removed from the jar with the spatula provided, the wax is very thick & extremely difficult to spread, so it's easy, if you rush, to make the layer too thick, which will mean that when you remove the cloth strip, the bulk of the wax will come off with it, but the hair & the thin layer you should have limited yourself to will stick to the skin!. So, spread some wax in one spot in a thick layer initially, then do the same on another spot on the skin. After 20-30 seconds or so, the skin will have warmed the wax in the earlier spot sufficiently that you will then be able to spread it in the very, very thin, nearly transparent layer that is what you NEED in order for the Nads to work! (2) Place the strip of cloth over the thinly waxed area, rub it on thoroughly, then -- IMPORTANT -- Let It Just Sit for another 30-40 seconds (or more) while you wax another area of the skin. I discovered, by accident, how very, very, very helpful it is to wait before removing. If I had to do it all over again, I would apply wax to half the leg, apply the strips & let them sit while I applied wax to the other half of the leg, strip the first half, let the second set of strips set while I applied wax to the first half of the 2nd leg, & so on... (3) The cloth strips provided are fine, but strips torn from an old sheet work even better. You cannot re-use a cloth strip until AFTER you have rinsed it clean AND dried it (this is essential to remember), so raid the ragbin! (4) Hold the skin TAUT while ripping the cloth strip off. This also makes a HUGE difference. (5) Pay attention to the direction in which you apply the Nad's wax (in the direction the hair grows) & the direction in which you rip off the cloth strip (the opposite direction). Summary: It took almost 50 minutes of trial & error to do my daughter's first leg correctly, 10 to do her second one. The second time we did it, it took a total ot 20 minutes for both legs & her skin was not the least bit irritated. I hope a few other folks will benefit from What We Learned!	\N	30	POSITIVE	4	31	\N	755	132	\N	\N
