1303	I am of two minds on this product. I bought it after my dentist highly recommended it and after I read two research papers on the device. It operates differently from other tooth brushes. If you want a demonstration of this, stand over a sink, fill your mouth with mouthwash and brush your teeth. I said \\\\"stand over a sink\\\\" because the Ultreo will create enough foam to dribble out your mouth and down your front. The brush is so powerful that, brushing he gums, you can feel the outline of your roots. Try that with another ultrasonic!     As the user manual states, this toothbrush works best if you use it with sufficient liquid. A critical problem, though, is that the brush has very few bristles and you cannot push those bristles far into the gaps in your teeth. Why? Because the guided-wave central transducer in the center of the brush head prevents the bristles from penetrating into the gaps, nooks and crannies in your teeth. (In fairness, I should note that the directions say to only brush using light pressure.)    This brush misses areas. After several weeks of using only this brush and floss, I tried my old ultrasonic and found blood on the bristles - clear evidence of missed areas. I would suggest that anyone using this brush use it in conjunction with a standard ultrasonic or an old fashioned v. 1.0 manual brush, so that the alternate brush compensates for the Ultreo's problem of shallow penetration of the bristles into gaps in the teeth.	\N	1	NEUTRAL	3	1	\N	1303	132	\N	\N
