1406	If you use these for single dosing and toss the syringe after that, my issue doesn't really affect you. I give my cat 1cc of an oral medication twice daily for a chronic condition, so want them to last for multiple uses--several week's worth, in fact.  So the only thing I'll mention here for everyone is that the syringe pictured is different at the tip from the \\\\"Luer Lock\\\\" these syringes have.  The Luer Lock has an extension of the side of the syringe barrel that comes down as far as the tip of the syringe, sort of like a surrounding shield, and has a spiral thread on the inside of the shield. For me, that means my cat would have to open her mouth quite a bit more to get the syringe tip inside--something I'm sure you'll appreciate that she does not! So I have to use a single edge razor blade to cut off the extending shield from each syringe. Time consuming to say the least, and it could be dangerous to surrounding fingers because of the pressure you need to exert to actually slice and roll the syringe while cutting.  So don't order these from the appearance of the tip in the photo, find out if \\\\"Luer Lock\\\\" is what you need, 'cause that's what you get, and it's very different from what you see pictured here. Sorry that I can't tell you what the unshielded tip is called (I didn't even know there were different types until these arrived on my door step).  I had been getting BD brand syringes w/o the needle tips from the vet for my cat's medication every time I got a refill, but I felt guilty taking their free syringes after I bought a large 12oz bottle of the med from them.  I'd get three syringes at a time from the vet, and rotate through that batch each time I medicated my cat. The BDs were good for about a month to six weeks before the rubber at the tip of the plunger finally started to harden, making it move in stops and starts instead of smoothly along the barrel of the syringe. This makes it very difficult to hit the exact mark and load a precise dosage, and whenthis started, I knew it was time to use fresh syringes. Syringes are cheap in 100 count boxes (and for me, provide about 3 year's worth at a month+ service life of the BDs), so I decided to order some from Amazon.  My first choice would have been BD syringes. Locally, BDs are widely available, but only with needles it seems, which I don't need. Amazon only had these, and I didn't see how they could be much different from the BDs, so that's what I ordered.  Unfortunately, the rubber in these syringes starts to harden much faster than the rubber on the tip of the BD plungers. In fact, the movement was never quite as smooth as that of a BD, even the first use fresh out of the packaging. I probably get two or three weeks out of a set of these before they start moving with more difficulty along the syringe barrel, instead of a month to six weeks with the BDs.  So, again, these would seem to be fine for single use. But I'd suggest BDs if you use syringes for multiple oral dosings.  And be sure \\\\"Luer Lock\\\\" is what you need before ordering!!!  Ron	\N	0	POSITIVE	4	0	\N	1406	132	\N	\N
