1505	I don't give 5 star reviews all the time because I think too many people rate products when they first get them and are excited and are more likely to overlook potential negatives. So when it came to shoe inserts, I decided to take the manufacturer's money back guarantee and try them for at least 2 weeks before deciding if I had a winner or not. I tried several inserts: Superfeet Green, Superfeet Black, Spenco Total Support, Sof Sole, and Orthoheel.<br />In the end, I kept 3 because I wear a lot of different shoes for a lot of different reasons. I'm workout a lot, ranging from bodybuilding/powerlifting in the gym to 3 mile runs, 10 mile hikes and field workouts involving sprints, throws, and jumps.I'm 6'2&#34; and weigh 240, and I'm hard on my footwear. I also have morton's foot structure. Even though my second toe isn't longer than my first, the second metatarsal head extends further than the 1st metatarsal head. This leads to the second, smaller metatarsal head taking on more stress than it can handle which can cause numbness and sharp pains.<br /><br />After trying them all, I found uses for 3 of them:<br /><br />The Black Superfeet were terrible. Almost zero support and they were hard on top of that. Sent them back after trying them several times hoping that I would like them. They were super low profile, so they will fit almost any shoe. But with no support and being hard, I didn't see any benefit vs adding nothing.<br /><br />The Sof Sole were good for adding cushion, but I felt they offered no support in the right place for my foot. Too much cushion without support in the right place leads to a mushy feeling for me, so I returned them.<br /><br />I kept the Superfeet Green wide version because they are the widest insert I can find. They fit perfectly into a pair of Altra Instinct shoes that I typically wear for leisure, walking the dogs, etc. I don't love them, but they are better than the inserts that came with those shoes so I kept them.<br /><br />The Spenco Total Support were a little better than the Superfeet Green for me. They also have a fairly wide forefoot and fit nicely into a pair of Asics Cumulus. Less support where I need it than the Orthoheel and not quiet as soft, but still more comfortable than the inserts that came with the shoes, so I kept them. I use the Asics mostly at the gym for treadmill and stepmill workouts.<br /><br />The Orthoheel were simply the best. That wasn't my immediate impression. I liked them at first but thought the support might be too much. But overall they offered the best cushion, soft without being mushy or too thick. As I tried them more, my feet adjusted to them and they quickly became my favorites. I now wear them in 3 different pairs of shoes and am ordering a second pair so I won't have to switch them out every time. I actually love a lot of the minimalist type shoes. I wear the Orthoheels in my New Balance MT10v2 which are very thin and flexible with no built in support at all. I also wear them in 2 pairs of Merrell minimalist shoes.<br /><br />After trying a lot of things to minimize foot pain and maximize athletic performance and daily comfort, I think I've found my slice of heaven. Shoes that are highly flexible and allow freedom of foot movement, coupled with Orthoheel to provide support only where I need it while adding a little softer landing with each step. The numbness, tingling and sharp pains in my left foot have been reduced to an occasional tingle if I land hard on my forefeet during workouts or hiking adventures. Other than that, my feet haven't felt so good in years!	\N	33	POSITIVE	5	35	\N	1505	132	\N	\N
