3286	[[ASIN:B0014ZFFFK Norelco 8150XL Speed-XL Shaver (factory refurbished)]]I really like this shaver. This is my third or fourth Norelco rotary shaver. Many years ago I gave up on shaving with a blade. Just negotiating the facial terrain with a blade without drawing blood was near impossible. I remember trying a foil shaver that was very irritating but the Norelco rotary was very comfortable. And the blades last pretty much the life of the shaver. The only problem with an electric shaver is dealing with humidity. Shaving in the winter is much easier. My last Norelco was one of the ones that is supposed to squirt out the Nivea lotion onto your face. Forget about it. The lotion squiting function broke down within the first year so I would just soap up my face a little before shaving. Then I would wash out the razor afterwards, but the blades eventually would get caked up with the soap and hair particles. Periodically I would have to take the blades out and clean them with a little brush. With this 8150XL, it is first of all very quiet. It holds the charge for 3 to 4 weeks, plus has an indicator that tells you when to charge. So no getting stuck in the middle of a shave with the battery running out. In addition, you don't have to mount the razor into a charger apparatus. The charger just plugs right into the razor. You can even shave while the razor is plugged  into the charger. It is washable, but I've decided not to try and soap up my face first on humid days. The cutting heads are mounted on a very cleverly engineered platform that bends with every curve, and I've been doing this long enough that I've found that I can get the job done without adding any soap into the equation.  This way the blades don't get caked up. It's just a matter of rinsing the razor off thoroughly after each shave to get the hair particles out. I've had it about two months now and the blades are as clean as new. The only other thing to mention is the new configuration of the cutting heads, 