3818	It has high ground clearance, a decent seat height, and good endurance.  This is not the same as the Rascal Fold-n-Go, though it's obviously from the same design.<br /><br />The most expensive difference is the battery: while the Rascal's three-prong (XLR) power charger will work with the LiteRider's battery, the battery packs themselves are not interchangeable.<br /><br />The most significant functional difference is the weight: the LiteRider's bottom board is twice as heavy as the Fold-n-Go, coming in at over 40 lbs.  With the battery, seat, and motor each weighing 20 lbs, the total unit's weight is 100 lbs.<br /><br />Size-wise, the seatpost tower does not detach from the bottom board.  That's one less piece to pack, but it also means the height of the bottom board is now increased.<br /><br />The tiller is well-designed.  'Delta tiller' means the handles are loops, not single posts.  This allows more flexibility - the user can hold the forward halves of the loop and push the throttle levers back with the thumbs, or hold the rear halves and pull the throttle levers back with the fingers.<br /><br />The tiller has a sway lock, which keeps it from swinging about when folded.  Additionally, the tiller will nest in the bottom tray, assuming it is not in use.<br /><br />All in all, it is very well designed.	\N	3	POSITIVE	4	3	\N	3818	132	\N	\N
