5001	When people are diagnosed with Sleep Apnea, they often think that they'll NEVER be able to get used to sleeping with a plastic mask strapped to their faces.  They often stop using their CPAP machine after the first couple of days or weeks, because they just can't stand it.<br /><br />Some sleep labs are still using the old-style masks, so if that's someone's experience of sleeping with a CPAP machine, it's not surprising that that person wants to forget the whole thing.<br /><br />But there's a new generation of masks for CPAP machines that are MUCH more comfortable than the old style.  I've used my CPAP machine for two years now, and I don't find it uncomfortable at all.  It was weird at first, but now I'm so used to it that it feels as if something's missing if I lie down without the mask on.<br /><br />This particular mask -- The Respironics Comfort Gel mask -- is the one I've been using for the past two years.  It's way more comfortable than I ever thought a plastic mask strapped to your head could be.  It's very soft around the face, and the forehead stop has a floating adjustment that makes it fit more easily and much more comfortable.  This is a truly comfortable mask.<br /><br />It's important to get the right size, and it's important to adjust the headgear properly.  If your mask is too loose, air will whistle out around the edges, which means you're not getting your full pressure.  Whistling air can also make a really loud noise, and having air blow into your eyes or across your cheek can be annoying.  If the mask is too tight, that also causes problems.  But if you adjust your headgear so that your mask is firmly held to your face but not tight, it works very well and is quite comfortable.<br /><br />Back sleepers have the easiest time wearing masks.  For side sleepers and stomach sleepers, it helps if you put your head at the very edge of your pillow, such that most of your head is supported by the pillow, but the very front edge of your face (which