5112	My 4 year old son has hypotonia (low tone) and some sensory issues (SPD).  I wanted to get a slant board to help him as he learns to write to assist him in refraining from the fisted grip.  This clipboard is highly portable, but it just was not sturdy enough for me.  The velcro just does not hold the board up unless I am right there with him working.  If alone, he will lean/push too hard and the whole thing comes down, which is frustrating for a kid who already struggles with writing.  Plus, he cannot rest his arm as he needs to because it is so narrow (barely bigger than the 8.5 inch width of the paper).  Also, the clip comes off easily and I have found it around the house.  I ended up using some Gorilla Glue to try to make the clip a permanent piece of the slant board.  I have opted to send it to school as an accommodation while he learns his letters and my husband is going to make him a solid, sturdy one from wood that is wide enough for home.  I think taking a three-inch binder and turning it backwards would have probably worked better than this... I used to do that with my special ed. kids who needed extra slant as \\\\"test\\\\" to see how they would benefit from a slant board.  My suggestion to parents/teachers is to try the binder test, then if you notice a marked improvement in handwriting and/or level of fatigue, investigate getting a \\\\"real\\\\" slant board that is sturdy and will hold up over time.	\N	2	NEGATIVE	2	2	\N	5112	132	\N	\N
