5269	I have painful arthritis in my upper thumb joint that causes pain when writing. This sounded like the solution, but is near worthless. My sense is that the company may have had good intentions but lacked an understanding of ergonomics as well as product testing and failed in the execution. Handwriting requires complex control over the instrument (more than just a pinch) and the Evo-Pen fails to deliver. It is a tiny, lightweight blob that results in chicken scratch writing, unrecognizable as your own. Writing is slow, too.  Forget taking notes during a lecture, for instance. It is also very cheaply made. If larger, better designed, heavier (which would allow better control), and with a good ink cartridge instead of the el cheapo included (which requires more bearing down and thus more pain than would a good cartridge with smooth ink flow), this could be a useful product. Granted it is not expensive and they offer a return guarantee, but the cost of returning it makes that pointless. Can't help wondering with all the money that must flow into the Arthritis Foundation why they don't design a true ergonomic, pain free writing instrument (not to imply the Evo-Pen is their product, this is just an aside comment). There is a real need for a good writing instrument for people with arthritis, be great if some industrial design school or devoted inventor would tackle the problem.	\N	1	NEGATIVE	1	1	\N	5269	132	\N	\N
