5443	One key requirement for using this is that you have to have a waist ... that is, an area just above your hips with a smaller circumference than your hips ... like the model shown in the product picture.  Otherwise, it will slip down and slide right past your hips.<br /><br />This is not a deficiency of the product, but a potential surplus of the user (me).<br /><br />You also have to have sufficient arm and shoulder strength to push down hard enough to stretch yourself.  I've been using the \\\\"Lynx\\\\" stretcher [[ASIN:B000PX1YDY Teeter Hang Ups P3 Back Stretcher]] for a few years: it attaches to the ankles, and when you push down on it, it pulls your legs away from your torso; with luck, the pelvis will follow the legs, thereby stretching the spine.<br /><br />In both cases, if the pelvis moves away from the torso, the spine will stretch; not a lot, but anything will help.  You have to try to relax everything except your arm muscles, and not resist the stretch.  That takes some practice.  It also helps to stretch slowly, and to relax/extend the stretch in a cyclical pattern, like interval training.<br /><br />When stretched, you can wiggle your hips, which often will produce a bit of a \\\\"release\\\\" in the spine.<br /><br />The results are not long-lasting, but with chronic back problems, nothing is long-lasting except the problem; and with a do-it-yourself device, you can repeat the stretch as often as you like, without paying someone for each visit.<br /><br />The instructions mostly show excercises done while standing up; but IMO, you must be lying down to do a stretch, or you will have your upper-body weight acting against the stretch.  Obviously, an inversion table is the best way to do any of this ... if you can tolerate being upside down, have the floor space, and can pay 10x what this belt costs.<br /><br />One con: the handles should have a larger diameter and better cushioning.  You have to push very hard to stretch yourself, and all that pressure is placed on thepalms of your hands.  Perhaps a pair of \\\\"split grips\\\\" (Amazon sells them for crutches) can be added to make the handles more comfortable.	\N	1	POSITIVE	4	1	\N	5443	132	\N	\N
