6449	Counterfeit toothbrush heads...who would imagine it, right?  Notice, however, the large number of users placing negative reviews of this products, noting that it fell apart after only three weeks.  I had the same experience.  After using the official Oral-B products, manufactured by Proctor and Gamble, in association with Braun, which held up well for months...I ordered these online.  Because I order things ahead, I didn't open these for a while.  When I did, I noticed that something was wrong...I thought it was a manufacturing defect.  After running through several heads (which took a few weeks each) I called Oral B / Proctor and Gamble.  They had me run a few checks / tests on the product, and found they were counterfeit.  By this time, it was too late to file a claim under Amazon's A-Z guarantee.  Fortunately, I was able to receive a refund in the form of a gift card by being persistent.  But I want to warn other users.  When you order this product, check immediately.  You can check if the product is counterfeit by taking a dime and trying very hard to scratch off the Oral-B logo on the toothbrush head.  Also, note that the counterfeit products arrived in a 4-pack, and Oral-B only sells 4 packs.  I ordered a 7-pack and it was suspicious when I got two 4-packs instead...but I didn't think much of it and just put them in my cupboard.  If you receive a counterfeit product, please contact the seller as well as Amazon and Oral B immediately.  It's not clear to me whether the seller is running the scam or the seller themselves was getting scammed by a supplier.  Either way, this situation is illegal and you can help to shut it down by being vigilant.	\N	0	NEGATIVE	1	0	\N	6449	132	\N	\N
