6542	My husband and I have been using this blood pressure monitor by Oregon Scientific for over 3 years now and it is still going strong. It is an excellent blood pressure monitor that takes the chore out of monitoring your blood pressure. We like that the measurements are taken from the wrist and that the adjustable band not only fits his large wrist at 8 ½ inches around, but also my small one at 5 ½ inches around. It measures systolic and diastolic blood pressure, mean arterial pressure, and pulse rate, and will store up to 60 time and date stamped measurements for one person or 30 for two people. It announces the measurements in a female voice in less than a minute and compares them to the World Health Organization standards telling you if your blood pressure is low, normal, slightly high, or high. The measurements are also visible as text on the LCD screen. It runs off of 2 triple A batteries and the first set are included with your new monitor. An indicator symbol appears on the LCD screen when the batteries are low.<br /><br />ADDITIONAL INFORMATION YOU MIGHT FIND USEFUL:<br />*Blood Pressure is the amount of force (pressure) that blood exerts on the walls of the blood vessels as it passes through them. Two pressures are measured for a blood pressure reading: Systolic blood pressure (the top number) measures the amount of pressure that blood exerts on vessels while the heart is beating. Diastolic blood pressure (the bottom number) measures the pressure in your vessels between heartbeats.<br />*Mean Arterial Pressure is the average pressure in the arteries during a complete cardiac cycle or a certain time interval.<br />*Pulse Rate is the number of times your heart beats each minute.<br /><br />You should monitor your blood pressure because it is your most crucial vital sign. If the force of blood pumping through your arteries is too high (known as hypertension), it will damage the smooth inner lining of your arteries leading to destructive inflammation and clotting. High blood pressure is the leading cause of heart attack, stroke, heart failure, kidney failure, and impotence. On the other hand, if your blood pressure is too low (known as hypotension), the blood is not able to bring oxygen and nutrients to the body's cells and remove waste matter. This may cause the cells to die.<br /><br />According to Dr. Mehmet Oz, you should check your blood pressure regularly because \\\\"high blood pressure is a silent killer. There are often no symptoms of this potentially deadly condition. So while you're going about your day, high blood pressure could be damaging your arteries, your heart, and other organs. The nasty consequences of leaving high blood pressure untreated include stroke, kidney damage, and even erectile dysfunction...Your risk of high blood pressure increases with age. If you smoke, are obese, are physically inactive, or have diabetes or high cholesterol, you may also be at an increased risk.\\\\"ng. High blood pressure is the leading cause of heart attack, stroke, heart failure, kidney failure, and impotence. On the other hand, if your blood pressure is too low (known as hypotension), the blood is not able to bring oxygen and nutrients to the body's cells and remove waste matter. This may cause the cells to die.<br /><br />According to Dr. Mehmet Oz, you should check your blood pressure regularly because \\\\"high blood pressure is a silent killer. There are often no symptoms of this potentially deadly condition. So while you're going about your day, high blood pressure could be damaging your arteries, your heart, and other organs. The nasty consequences of leaving high blood pressure untreated include stroke, kidney damage, and even erectile dysfunction...Your risk of high blood pressure increases with age. If you smoke, are obese, are physically inactive, or have diabetes or high cholesterol, you may also be at an increased risk.\\\\"	\N	2	POSITIVE	5	2	\N	6542	132	\N	\N
