7295	I tried a couple different types of humidifiers before buying this one about a year ago; an untrasonic cool mist that made everything near it wet or clammy, and a warm mist that boils water and emits steam. Neither of those distribute the moist air very far into a room because they have no fan. The Essick MA0800 is one of two evaporative cool mist units I purchased and it has clearly worked very well for two winters now, able to bring the humidity level in our very large den up to 40-45 % very quickly. The built-in auto shut off (turns off the fan when it reaches a designated humidity level, and turns back on when it drops to a certain level) works well but, since the sensor is close to the unit, I recommend a quality digital hygrometer be placed across the room to be sure of what your actual humidity level is. The only drawback to this unit is that the fan is a bit noisy on medium speed and very noisy on high. I suggest turning the unit on medium or high speed a half hour (or an hour) before you're planning on spending extended time in the room and then cutting the fan speed back to low once you're in there; that will usually hold the humidity at a good level for a long time with negligible fan noise. (The fan is slightly quieter on a Sunbeam SCM 3609 that I purchased for our large bedroom, which also works well, but the auto shut-off is not as good as the Essick's.)	\N	2	POSITIVE	5	2	\N	7295	132	\N	\N
