8956	I am a physician and researcher with a mother who died of breast cancer, so I cannot take HRT. I have not been given any alternatives by my doctor, so I did some research. I learned that most current HRT products are made from plants, usually yams, and thus they contain phytoestrogens. I could not find a reliable yam supplement, so looked at soy. This Healthy Woman product has been chemically analyzed by independent researchers who had nothing to do with the company. The researchers published a paper showing the product truly contains the amount of soy derivatives that the label states. This is important to me because the FDA does not regulate supplements' claims of efficacy, or the amounts of ingredients claimed on the labels. As a physician working with my own patients, and as a patient myself, I advise using supplements only from reliable companies - even better if research has been done using the supplement, since there is so little regulation of this industry. So, I tried this product and find it works wonders, although it initially took about a month to reduce my symptoms. If I forget to take it for 3 or 4 days, the symptoms return, reinforcing my conclusion that it actually works. However, if it truly works, it probably does contain phytoestrogens - so I think it is important to check with your doctor before starting this product. Good luck!  PS: search \\\\"NCCAM supplements\\\\" for more information on supplement research.	\N	0	POSITIVE	5	0	\N	8956	132	\N	\N
