9262	This is a really well-built, nicely designed chair which is let down by totally inadequate front rigging.  The chair itself is comfortable and easy to maneuver, and quite lightweight.  It has features like a back pocket and a rear step tube for lifting the front casters over an irregularity, both lacking on our more expensive InvaCare chair.  The problem is with the swingaway footrests.  The way these normally work is that an L-shaped tube has the actual footrest attached to the short part of the L, with the long part fitting inside a slightly larger support tube which is the more-or-less vertical part of the front rigging (although it is usually on a slant).  On most normal chairs, the L-shaped part is locked into place with screws, push-in buttons, twist locks or some other device which keeps the footplates oriented while the chair is in use.  This chair lacks such locking devices, depending entirely on a friction fit of one tube inside another.  As a result, it is easy for the footplates to twist inwards and foul the front casters.  If you are handy with tools, it should be possible to drill the two tubes and insert a nut and bolt to hold the footplates in place; we elected to return the chair to Amazon.  Incidentally, I'm not sure where the MedMobile name comes from; the carton had a Chinese name and model number.	\N	0	NEGATIVE	2	0	\N	9262	132	\N	\N
