10922	I purchased this pedometer from another site (for more money) back in July, and six months later it still works amazingly well.  Like the instructions say, I keep it upright (top with keychain connection towards my head) in a tight pocket on my hip, and it is still very accurate all this time later.  Of course if you shake it around it will count steps - that is how the product works.  The up and downward movement is translated as walking, so if it isn't reasonably snug against you, the extraneous movement of it bouncing around will be counted.  If you just want to wear it around all day and see how many steps you normally take, remember that driving over bumpy roads can register quite a few steps.  If you wear it around your neck and under your shirt, quick upper body motions, such as washing your hands, will register as steps as well.    If you use the product in the right way (in a snug pocket etc) you can get very accurate, consistent results. I've been doing the same hour walk for the last six months and the steps never vary more than 4% of the total (with my average 6000 step walk, it can vary between 5800 to 6200, but that also reflects how large my stride was that day).  I got one for my friend who walks with me now, and the results of both pedometers are never farther than 100 or so steps, which can also be explained by stride length.  Overall, a great pedometer that I strongly recommend, especially for the price and convenience.	\N	0	POSITIVE	5	0	\N	10922	132	\N	\N
