11164	I've had my Philips HQ9100 for two years so far as i type this review, and life is good.    I was very, very tired of shavers dying due to dying/dead rechargeable, not-easily-replaceable batteries: more junk going to e-waste, more wasted resources, more poor performance, annoyance, and unnecessary shopping. I am always near power when shaving, and had/have no need for a battery-powered shaver, yet that was pretty much all that was in the U.S. market, other than a couple of low-end models!    Thanks to Amazon and Mercury Shaver Centre, i was finally able to find a higher-end corded shaver: the Philips HQ9100. Why this model or one like it was (is?) not directly marketed in the U.S. is beyond me... nice unit. The rest of this review consists of some notes i made at the one-month point, updated where needed, and some current thoughts at the two-year mark.    Though i have been shaving since the mid 1970s, and always with electrics, this has been my very first rotary shaving head experience. Since this is the only rotary shaver i have ever used, i will not be able to compare it to other models. Most of my previous shavers have been various Braun models, with the most recent being one of the Panasonic wet/drys (a feature i never used)--all linear cutters.    It may also help to know that i am an infrequent shaver, as i dislike shaving. 3 to 4 days between shaves is typical for me.    ************  **First Impressions  The first thing that struck me about the HQ 9100 upon opening it is that this model is still made in Holland (amazingly). Build quality seems decent. The carrying case is nice, though i do wish they had a separate holder for the cleaning brush, which has to float around with the power cord. The case is cloth with a Velcro® latch. Everything is holding up well after two years.    The first thing that struck me upon plugging in the HQ 9100 and turning it on was \\\\"Wow! This thing is QUIET!\\\\" As in about 70 dB almost touching the unit, and about 57 dB 1/2 meteraway. This was measured with a Radio Shack 33-2055 Sound Level Meter. Interestingly the numbers were pretty much identical with both C and A weighting. Whatever the numbers may or may not mean to you, this is far and away the quietest electric shaver i have ever heard anyone use anywhere at any time.    **Shaving  I have indeed found that the shaving experience with this rotary head shaver is different, for me, than with the linear shavers i have previously used. First, this is most definitely the closest shave i have ever had, though a couple of the better Brauns were nearly as close when brand-new. However, to get this nice, close shave takes longer than the linear-head shavers i have previously used. I don't particularly mind the extra time, since this shaver is so much easier on the ears and the end result is better.    I did had trouble with hair-pulling early on, due to my propensity to let the whiskers grow long between each shave. As my technique improved over the months, and with shaving sooner before the hairs get ultra-long, this is no longer a problem. On the rare occasion where i shave \\\\"like a normal person\\\\" before the whiskers get abnormally long, there are no pulling problems. And, the pulling is nowhere near as bad as a Remington linear i tried circa the late 1980s (when Victor Kiam [sp?] was owning and advertising the brand) which seemed to enjoy yanking my beard hairs out (that one went right back to the store for a refund). While i have not bothered to measure the speed, qualitatively it seems that the rotary cutters on this Philips are moving more slowly than the linear cutters i am used to using, which may be part of my problem. Taking a deep breath and being less manic about shaving (moving more slowly) has reduced the pulling to zero most of the time.    The 3 rings of cutters in each cutting head definitely seem to work well, and i would guess much faster and better than the older single-ring design. The independent suspension for each head and the extra degrees of motion available from the SmartTouch XL suspension system certainly do allow the heads to follow my (many) face contours quite well. So far, i have the sensitivity control cranked all the way to N (Normal), and have not experimented with any settings towards S (Sensitive).    **Cleaning  Easy... i don't even need the brush for the daily cleanings! Running the shaver under hot water with the head assembly open, closing the assembly and shaking, then re-opening the assembly to let it dry (the procedure recommended by Philips) is fast, easy, and working wonderfully for me.    **Cutter Replacement  It is now time, and i am soon to make the order. I have had the cutters out for the more in-depth every-6-months cleaning: easy... just be sure to keep them in order so they go back into the same place (per the manual, for optimal shaving). Many of the interior mechanical parts appear to be plastic, which may be necessary to dampen vibrations and keep this unit as wonderfully quiet as it is.    **The Cord  Words cannot describe how thrilled i am to no longer need to be concerned about the state of charge of my shaver, nor whether or not the battery is dying and dragging down the motor via loading the power supply! Shaving is now much less of a dreaded chore... something i am willing to do more often, making everyone who has to look at me happy. :-)    **Summary  I am expecting this shaver to continue to provide excellent service for years to come. I highly recommend it to anyone wanting a great shave and the ability to escape the scourge of batteries and ongoing new shaver shopping! I am giving it 4 stars instead of 5 due to the tendencies for hair-pulling, which were less with the Brauns and the only thing about those units which was superior, and the tendency for the cleaning brush to get mangled swimming loose in the storage case (i removed it and store it separately).	\N	0	POSITIVE	4	0	\N	11164	132	\N	\N
