11538	I've been an ER RN for some 12 years and have gone through a few stethoscopes in my time. Oddly, none have been stolen or lost usually the tube just goes catatonic from my apparently toxic neck oil. Both Littmann and ADC will replace the tubing like once for this but in the higher end scopes like the Cardiology's if one has to come up with the bucks it's like $60 to $90 so it's cheaper to just get a cheaper stethoscope. Fact is most of us in healthcare and Nursing Students in particular really don't need a top notch Cardiology monster. It's cool but when one pulls 12 hour shifts they get plain heavy.  Fact is there is much to be said for a decent \\\\"light\\\\" stethoscope that can be had for around $40, first you won't feel half as bad as when your $150 fancy scope goes AWOL. Look Littmann makes a fine stethoscope and like Green Giant Niblet Corn (ho ho ho)they get premo shelf space for their advertisement dollar. Look there is not a damn thing wrong with Littmann, a fine product but ADC is just as good in many people's opinion and will save you some money, plus ADC tends to have a longer warranty. This scope here is really a decent single head design which over the years I have come to prefer with subtle sound differences that can be ascultated by varying pressure. In truth in the ER I'm just into the basics (now) and I want a decent light weight scope. My only complaint is the tubing might be a bit long, handy when one has a rather odiferous patient, more a problem just hanging on my neck. If I wasn't so lazy I'd lop off 1.75 inches, problem is if that's too short I'm screwed while being a bit too long I can always fix. A very decent stethoscope, more than up to the task for over 90% of all healthcare providers at a cost that won't break the bank.	\N	11	POSITIVE	4	13	\N	11538	132	\N	\N
