11666	I have one 421a and three 582a's. I love these purifiers! They are very easy to maintain and inexpensive to replace the filters and effective in removing dust and odors. Only 45.00 for the hepa filter which lasts long because there's a prefilter. Just vacuum out every so often. Uses very little electricity on low. Would definitely recommend. I've owned several other purifiers(3M filtrete, holmes, honeywell) and the rabbit air's are much better all around.  As far as the carbon filter, this is one of the best carbon filters on the market, pellets work much better then mesh (such as winix carbon filters).   These filters are actually REFILLABLE, I refill mine every couple months(probably overkill). Use a knife and carefully pry the top  cover off, it is spot glued. Once off then I discard the used carbon and refill with new carbon(kinda tedious but well worth it). I use carbon from bulkreefsupply.com for only $10 for half gallon/1.5lbs (special grade bituminous carbon). I get about 5 refills per container(for only $10!) I then use small zip ties to reinstall the cover.(theres probably a better/easier way but dont have the time to create one). Let me know if anyone has any better ideas than this. (All n all 5 refills for $10 or replace for 29.95 X 5 = $149.75)  (Ps I am not affiliated with bulkreefsupply.com, research shows they have very high quality carbon for the price)  As far as cleaning it - the most effective way to re activate carbon is very high heat and low oxygen(but not to low). According to research recommended temperatures are up to 2000 degrees and with a certain level of oxygen so it does not burn. Some people just put the carbon in an oven to re activate it but you would obviously need to remove it from the plastic casing in which the filter is made of. Washing these filters will not work which is why they smell. You need to \\\\"cook\\\\" them lol. Google regenerating activated carbon or reusing activated carbon.	\N	3	POSITIVE	5	4	\N	11666	132	\N	\N
