13067	I recently purchased the MIGHTY MACK rollator after a stay in Physical Therapy rehab. I am recovering from a broken femur. I won't say \\\\"hip fracture\\\\" because everyone seems to think that only the elderly break their hips.<br /><br />I am a plus-size person, and I knew that my recovery would be slow and gradual. As I improve and grow stronger, I knew I would need to take it slow and be able to rest as I began walking again. In rehab I tried standard walkers, and then tried a \\\\"rollator\\\\". There was no contest. Surprisingly, the cost of a GOOD rollator is not that much higher than a standard folding walker frame, when acquired through insurance. I was, however, outraged at the charge at \\\\"retail\\\\" to my insurance-- $550+. When I found the exact same rollator at Amazon for $256, and I have seen it online elsewhere for as low as $158.<br /><br />The walker provides a great transition and support for those who need a little extra support while walking. It has an approximate 25\\\\" wide span between the handles and a nice padded seat for resting along the way. The brakes are responsive and provide a \\\\"locked\\\\" position, as well as an on-demand breaking action. The handlebars have a rubber ergonomically-shaped grip that allows the user to lean hard onto the handles and provides a nice comfortable grip.<br /><br />I am broad in the beam, and the seat fits me well. I love my rollator, and it is great when you have to wait in line somewhere. I have also been grateful for the included basket carrier, and I have used the wide seat as a \\\\"tray\\\\" carrier at the buffet, or for extra space for hauling groceries.<br /><br />I have the blue rollator, which is really a deep steel-blue, it almost looks black, unless you are in the sunlight, where you can see the blue shades. I wish it came in purple :)<br /><br />I will mention a few of the drawbacks that I have discovered.<br /><br />* It was designed for big people, so it is a big, heavy device (28 lbs). I have a Mercury Marquis vehicle, and this device \\\\"JUST\\\\" fits in the trunk-- but not much else will fit. It takes up the whole space. This model allows you to remove the basket and support back bar-- and it does fold \\\\"slightly\\\\" flatter-- but the width of the seat does not collapse for transport. Once folded, I place the vehicle in the truck with the seat facing down, handlebars up. One tip-- loosen the handlebar screws to fold the handles toward the center.<br /><br />* Note to the manufacturer-- big does not necessarily mean TALL. I am 5'6\\\\" and my rollater seat is just slightly too tall for me. When I sit, my feet do dangle about 1/2 inch off the ground. The handlebars have an adjustable height, but the height of the seat is not adjustable. Another thing-- I'm short, but I usually wear shin-to-ankle-length dresses. My skirt is ALWAYS getting caught up in the back wheels. Be prepared to pin up a skirt or wide pant-leg that could get caught. Simple solution--stop, backup and go again. (Just a minor annoyance).<br /><br />* I accidentally left my rollator in front of the window-- the sun heated the foam seat and it warped slightly-- the heat in the trunk of my car (Texas summertime) also softens the foam cushion parts on the vehicle and if they are mushed against anything in the trunk-- it causes strange, permanent indentations.<br /><br />* Be careful on uneven surfaces. The brake assembly only affects the back wheels (directly under the handlebars).  IF one of the two back wheels doesn't touch the ground, both brakes are not effective in either regular or locked mode. I have had my \\\\"one brake\\\\" stroller do a little pivot spin on a slanted surface (such as at the movies). I was already sitting down in the seat-- but my rollator slipped away from my reach.<br /><br />DON'T GET ME WRONG-- overall my \\\\"hotwheels\\\\", as I call them, have been my saving grace. My job requires me to travel LONG hallways. I always take my rollator and my cane. I use the rollator to get where I want to go, and then use the cane in tight spaces. People ask me why I didn't just get a scooter, it would have been easier--but I think if I get use to \\\\"not walking\\\\" it will have a poor effect on my life. Keep walking as long as you can!<br /><br />FUN ACCESSORIES: the flyer that came with my rollator offers fun and colorful seat covers, horns, lights, reflectors, bells, Cup holder, Tote bag, Basket cover, etc. I went to the local \\\\"MegaLoMart\\\\" and bought my own accessories from the bike section. I was tempted to put pink irridescent streamers on the handlebars, but I held myself back. I did buy a pair of fingerless bike gloves that I used during the first month. If you've never used a walker or cane-- you may not realize that the skin on your palms will peel until your hands toughen up. (I recommend the gloves that have gel pads on the palm.)<br /><br />Try this one out. IF you can't get it through your insurance-- dig deep online- you can find a fair price (be wary of low price/high shipping fees-- always double-check when you think you have found a good deal).<br /><br />Happy Rollating!<br /><br />[NOTE TO THE WORLD: I have recently discovered that MANY supposedly ADA-accessible bathrooms just don't cut it. I don't know how many times I have entered the stall and can't bring my rollator in with me (how do folks manage with a wheelchair and who need space to transfer?). I urge everyone to speak up to managers of public spaces that don't comply with accessible standards.]ier--but I think if I get use to \\\\"not walking\\\\" it will have a poor effect on my life. Keep walking as long as you can!    FUN ACCESSORIES: the flyer that came with my rollator offers fun and colorful seat covers, horns, lights, reflectors, bells, Cup holder, Tote bag, Basket cover, etc. I went to the local \\\\"MegaLoMart\\\\" and bought my own accessories from the bike section. I was tempted to put pink irridescent streamers on