13432	Many laundry detergents will cause terrible contact dermatitis if my clothing or linens are washed in them. I even avoid walking down the laundry detergent aisle at the grocery store because it causes me to sneeze and makes my eyes water. These Dropps laundry detergent pacs are scent- and dye-free with only a mild, non-perfumey odor and they don't cause me any problems at all.<br /><br />Dropps contains no enzymes, either, which are good at removing organic stains but which can harm some clothing fibers, particularly those that are protein-based, such as wool and silk. This makes Dropps ideal for machine-washable wool clothing. It may be a consideration if you have a lot of organic stains but it is best to pre-treat organic stains with a stain product containing enzymes, anyway.<br /><br />The pouches are surprisingly compact with each containing about 0.39 ounce or 11.5 g. They are supposed to go directly into the washing drum, not into the detergent dispenser. The high-efficiency commercial washers have a short pre-wash cycle so I put the pacs close to the edge of the main cycle dispenser anyway and they seem to dissolve fine in the blast of hot water that runs through it.<br /><br />The package recommends two pouches for large loads but one seems to get the usual load of laundry clean even in the large triple-loaders. For the occasional load of heavily-soiled wash, I also put a tablespoon of powdered detergent in the pre-wash cycle dispenser and use a laundry booster pac in the main cycle. I always use hot water and a small amount of either oxygen- or chlorine-based bleach for sanitizing the laundry because the washing machines are shared.<br /><br />Ingredient information from package:<br /><br />Biodegradable plant-based surfactants (anionic and nonionic). Phosphate-free, NPE-free and safe for septic tanks. No artificial whitening agents.<br /><br />Ingredient list from manufacturer's website:<br /><br />Laureth - 7 (Plant derived + biodegradable cleaning