13504	This stuff is in a tube, and gets squeezes out much like toothpaste, except it is very smooth and creamy. It sticks to skin and immediately begins soaking in. You will need soap and water to get it off your fingers. Little to no fragrance.<br /><br />My experience: When used daily on hard foot skin, the skin actually softens.<br />Before, I had hard skin around my heels that was cracking in places.<br />Before, I had hard skin on the outside of the ball of my feet.<br />Before, I had hard skin on the inside of my big toes and various places on other toes.<br /><br />I applied this balm by putting just a small dab (you know, more than a pinch, but less than a ¼ teaspoon.) on my finger and then lightly rubbing it onto the hard places. Rub it for a few seconds and wipe away all the excess to go to the next place. After a few days, things were more pliable. Within a couple of weeks, all of the areas were softer, and the cracks in my heels were smaller and going away. It seems to work better if applied after a shower, but I don't always wait for a shower to use it. I now use it on any area that starts getting hard again.<br /><br />I have been very amazed at how well it works. I have been using it for about 6 months, including through the winter, and my feet are in great shape, better than they have been for years. I cannot believe how much softer the skin is. And my first 4oz tube is about ¾ used--it goes a long ways.<br /><br />I have peripheral neuropathy which identifies me with those who have diabetes, have problems controlling blood sugar levels, am morbidly obese, and generally in poor health. I have less pain in my feet because of the softer skin absorbs bumps in my shoes and walking surfaces. YES, LESS PAIN! This is not a cure for peripheral neuropathy and it does not heal the nerves. But softer feet hurt less. If this stuff will work for me, it will probably work for most anyone.<br /><br />-- I have nothing to gain in any way from this favorable review. 