14308	Being used to the clean feeling after using ultrasonic toothbrushes, I recently ran into a problem: I was traveling to a country with a different voltage, so I couldn't take my regular toothbrush with me. Other battery powered brushes I tried just didn't do the trick. Then I found the B410 for $20 in a big box store and decided to give it a try. Now I'm in love.<br />The advantages: It's powered by two regular AA batteries. You can get these in virtually any convenience store in the world. No need to take a charger with you, and you can brush your teeth in the wilderness where there are no power outlets. You can remove the batteries when you air travel, to avoid switching on the toothbrush accidentally (TSA opens suitcases that make a buzzing sound...). It comes with a protection cap for the brush head that stays firmly in place. It's quite compact.<br />But how does it perform? Flawlessly. The brush is soft enough to not hurt the gums, but firm enough to be effective. With brushes like these, you need to just gently touch your gums and teeth, anyways. I don't know if the \\\\"Hydroclean\\\\" part makes any difference. But this little thing cleans better and is more convenient to use than my big, regular ultrasonic toothbrush. I ended up using it even after the vacation. So far I'm using the same batteries it came with, and after more than two weeks of brushing for three minutes twice daily I haven't noticed a decline in power yet.<br />It's simple, it's effective, it's cheap. I wonder if Philips is aware that this little wonder might have an impact on the sales of their premium line of toothbrushes for $100+. After all they just add gimmicks to them to justify the price, not sure if people will feel these are worth paying five times the price.	\N	2	POSITIVE	5	2	\N	14308	132	\N	\N
