14327	I bought this Rolling Cane to use in my house when keeping my Grandchildren. The 3 wheeled walker I had bought to use when I went out, swiveled way too much & wasn't safe on ramps. I didn't have the hand strength to operate the brakes. When my condition worsened to the point I needed something in the house too, I used the 3 wheeled walker in the house until I started keeping my Grandchildren. The brakes on the walker were sharp metal that could be dangerous for children...it was also just in the way...so I began my search for something that would help me get around the house safely without being a hazard to the children.      While searching for a quad cane to use instead, I found this Rolling Cane. It's OK & I've found it to be useful enough that I chose to keep it. You have to be careful not to put your hand in the front or it will tip. I almost fell when it tipped as I placed it on carpet. The front swivel wheels were turned sideways so when I pushed it tipped forward. After that I was uneasy with it for awhile but gained confidence after learning the proper way to use it. Put your hand on the back where the push down brake is. It's not as likely to tip that way. When going from tile to carpet, keep it rolling & it will move smoothly over the carpet. Do NOT lift it & place it on the carpet or the wheels may get turned sideways. The brake engages easily just by pushing down on the cane, which is good for someone with weak hands.      I do use it when going out places where not much walking is required but only if I have my husband there to hold onto. It's better than taking the walker to restaurants. Going down ramps without help with anything that rolls doesn't work for me. Now that just holding my husband's arm wasn't enough anymore, the Rolling Cane is useful to me. It may work even better for someone needing less support than I & needing it only for balance. I have a progressive condition & waited until I was pretty bad before trying anything. I also don't know if it's any better than a quad cane & it's much more expensive. If you are in such bad shape that lifting a cane is a problem then this may not offer enough support. I don't think it would roll well on grass & when I've taken it on outings I have to really pay attention to cracks & bumps in pavement & doorways. I use a 4 wheeled walker in my yard. It rolls well on the grass, unlike the 3 wheeled walker. I can't operate the brakes on the 4 wheeled walker either but it doesn't roll away from you on grass. The 3 wheeled walker is getting donated because it's not of any use to me anymore. I bought a basic walker to use in the house when I'm tired & need more support than the Rolling Cane or I need the basket on the walker to carry things like laundry that are difficult to carry while using the Rolling Cane.      Bottom line: This Rolling Cane may prove useful to you depending on your condition & where you plan to use it. Since I've never used a regular cane, I can't really compare the two. If I had it to do over, I would try a quad cane first since it would be much cheaper. It is nice that I don't have to lift the Rolling Cane since I can't stand well without holding on to something...a quad cane may not have worked for me either. My experience has been that I just have to try everything to see what works best & I still need different things for different situations. The Rolling Cane is worth a try & I guess you can return it if it doesn't help you. I'm fortunate to be able to afford trying everything & I just donate what doesn't work for me anymore.	\N	0	NEUTRAL	3	0	\N	14327	132	\N	\N
