14919	I've used these earmuffs for about 4 days now.  They came with a nice foam filled impact plastic carrying case.  I find that they work fairly similarly to the NCT Pro-Active earmuffs I had in the past, although their passive component seems a little better.  The choice to use a regular AA battery and avoid custom batteries that require another wall-wart is a nice improvement also.  The active component is primarily useful with low frequencies, but if you work on or around running diesel engines, like I do, then the active component is quite useful.  My experience is that if something is going wrong in an engine room, the noise it makes will usually be a high frequency sound, and by canceling the low frequency sounds, these muffs can unmask sounds that provide an early warning for problems.  I also find that the plug for the 1/4\\\\" stereo input jack dangles, but appearance isn't a big issue in my industry.  The stereo input jack is a nice option, although I seldom use it.  I've often thought that with a properly selective amplifier plugged into that jack, these type of muffs could give good competition to the Peltor brand Tactical muffs.  The biggest downside to these muffs for me is their weight.  Wearing them for more than 10 hours a day can be challenging, although I guess that's true of any muffs to some extent.  Note:  After using these for a year now, I'd add the following words:  The plastic of the cushions got hard faster than those of the (green) Peltor muffs do, although it looks like gluing on Peltor replacement cushions will likely work ok.  I've found the function provided by the stereo input to be quite useful in enabling me to hear people calling on a portable VHF radio while I'm in noisy machinery spaces.  One caveat however: Most VHFs are monaural, and the weight of a mono patch cord plus a Radio Shack mono-stereo adapter is enough to make it fall out a lot.  I've been able to use a mono patch cord w/o the adapter by putting a little o-ring on it for aspacer, but that generally wants to fall out also.  It'd be nice if the next version of these had both mono and stereo inputs, and maybe some sort of cord clip or strain relief, so one could wear them while connected to a VHF for a machinery space work shift.	\N	1	POSITIVE	4	1	\N	14919	132	\N	\N
