15359	I've used these BCA units from Ya-Man for my fitness clients for several years with great success. <br /> <br />One important thing to keep in mind about bio-impedence fitness analysis equipment - the scales only measure your body composition from the waist down (up one leg, through the gut & down the other leg) and this BCA unit measures from the waist up (up one arm, across the torso and out the other arm.  The scales and BCAs run this data through a formula to \\\\"approximate\\\\" the total body composition results.  Thus what I've done is run both tests for my fitness clients and average the two together.  This works great. <br /> <br />The nice thing about the BCA is it's portability and low cost and the results are within +/- 2% of the scales so they appear to by quite accurate. <br /> <br />My comments on the previous review: (1) early in their development Ya-man had some issues with erroneous data errors because they based some of their computations on statistics based on the Japanese population which is vastly differnent than our overweight U.S. population.  These errors were fixed via the use of U.S. fitness data in the 2nd generation of the BCA.  Chances are the previous reviewer either received one of these earlier units OR they failed to follow the directions: <br /> <br />To get good results it is important to be adequately hydrated, barefoot and standing on a solid surface with hands outstretched in front of you.  If not properly hydrated the results can vary significantly.	\N	1	POSITIVE	5	1	\N	15359	132	\N	\N
