15770	I rec'd my ECG meter about two weeks ago. It's very easy to use and when your heart is in normal sinus rhythm, you get a smiley face! I had open heart surgery in June, '10 for a hole in my heart (ASD); I developed atrial flutter after I was taken off Amiodarone to control my rhythm. I also had an electrical cardioversion a few weeks ago; this meter helps me to make sure I am still in sinus rhythm. You just need to spend a few minutes reading the small manual and setting up your profile. (You can actually have multiple users for one device.)<br /><br />The device records up to 150 readings and stores them in its memory. If you want more readings, you can download your older readings to your PC using the software that comes with the monitor to clear the device's memory. It creates a spreadsheet of your readings. However, you don't need to use your PC if you don't want to.<br /><br />There are no leads/stickers for your skin. You merely wet your hands, dry them so they are just moist, then hold the device in your right hand with the contact points touching your index finger as you also press the monitor on your left palm on a second set of contact points (you can also place it under your left breast instead of your left palm if that works better). That's all there is to it! In 30 sec., you have your digital waveform in the window of the device, heart rate, and (hopefully) smiley face! It's never failed to give me a reading.<br /><br />A great tool! If you are dealing with heart rhythm issues, this is worth the investment--for peace of mind, if nothing else. It will also provide valuable data for your cardiologist to review.<br /><br />P.S. In one of the details of the instrument above, it says, \\\\"100 records of ECG Strips, each record with 30-second ECG waveform and analysis result.\\\\" This sounds like there are paper strips that come with. There are not. It means it is just showing you a strip on the LCD screen. There no paper with this machine.	\N	10	POSITIVE	5	11	\N	15770	132	\N	\N
