15822	The rod does indeed add a good amount of elbow room to your shower.  I was actually surprised and the extra room exceed my expectations.  The fact the rod is adjustable and pivots at the mounting bracket made the rod my top choice in the price range.  However, the installation may require some patience and the hardware included may have been incomplete if I where mounting the rod on studs.    Mounting on dry wall you will need a 5/16 drill bit, per the instructions.  This is the safest installation.    Mounting on tile, you will need a 5/16 MASONRY drill bit (in order to cut through tile).  This is how I installed the rod and had to order masonry drill bits from amazon for $20.  There were cheaper ones, but I figured I might as well go for a good reviewed item as to not delay the installation even further.  I would start will smaller bits and work up to 5/16 to avoid cracking the tile.  Also, the rod includes paper templates that tape to the wall to guide your drilling.  Since the tile under the paper template is slippery, the drill may slip on you.  I put clear tape over the template hole marks to help keep the drill tip in place.    Mounting on a stud.... ???  The directions are not clear at all what to do here.  The hardware included just had wall anchors, which I assume don't work in studs.  I guess you will need to find the appropriate screws on your own.    Also, I used an electric drill for the screws/wall-anchor.  This was a mistake because the wall-anchors are cheap and the treads got worn out easily.   Finally, note that since the rod extends away from your tub, the liner will need a little extra slack to compensate.  The further you mount the rod, the more slack you will need.  Keep that in mind before installing.	\N	0	NEUTRAL	3	0	\N	15822	132	\N	\N
