15838	Unfortunately, while ferrets LOVE these treats, they may put ferrets' health at risk. Ferrets are carnivorous, requiring quality proteins and fats, and sugar and carbohydrates are exceptionally bad for ferrets and puts them at great risk for a condition called insulinoma. Before we knew about diet and insulinoma in ferrets, we treated our ferrets to these per the instructions on the package and never really read the ingredients, trusting that a company marketing a product for ferrets would know what is safe and healthy to feed them. At about 4-5 years of age, all of our ferrets began displaying some strange symptoms: wobbly gait, staring off into space, increased urination, and later lethargy, extreme weight loss, inability to stand or walk at all, glazed eyes, and eventually a slow, painful death after 1-2 months of symptoms. It was horrifying, and agonizing to watch and have veterinarians say there is nothing that can be done at this stage. After consulting with many veterinarians after multiple ferrets displayed these same symptoms, we were able to narrow down exactly what was going on: insulinoma, caused by supplementing our ferret's diets with treats containing sugar and grains. We beat ourselves up over having caused our ferrets to live shortened lives by 1-2+ years just because we wanted to spoil them. These treats contain sugar as the first ingredient, which ferrets cannot properly metabolize. PLEASE, read up on the condition called insulinoma in ferrets and its link to sugar and carbohydrates in their diet, and come to an educated decision on what to feed your ferrets. We wish we had.	\N	1	NEGATIVE	1	1	\N	15838	132	\N	\N
